<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Horn and East Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Jimma</h1><p class="st-meta">1790 CE – 1932 CE (approx.) · capital Jiren</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The richest of the Oromo kingdoms of the Gibe region, famous for its coffee and markets, which kept autonomy within Ethiopia until 1932.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Jimma, also called Jimma Abba Jifar, emerged around 1790 when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oromo">Oromo</a> groups in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibe-river">Gibe</a> valley, who had settled there during the migrations of earlier centuries, formed a monarchy under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abba-magal">Abba Magal</a>. It grew by absorbing neighbours, and under Abba Jifar I (r. 1830 to 1855) the dynasty adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The king ruled through appointed governors and officials, a departure from the egalitarian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oromo">Oromo</a> gadaa system. Jimma's markets, especially at Hirmata near the capital Jiren, traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, civet, gold and slaves. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abba-jifar-ii">Abba Jifar II</a> (r. 1878 to 1932) submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelik-ii">Menelik II</a> in 1882 and paid tribute in return for internal autonomy.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abba-jifar-ii">Abba Jifar II's</a> death in 1932 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a> ended Jimma's autonomy and made it a province of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a>. Abba Jifar's palace at Jiren is preserved, and Jimma is now one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia's</a> main <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> towns.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-jimma.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Jimma" width="320" height="289"><figcaption>Image: LeGabrie · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gibe_kingdoms.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1790 CE – 1932 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Jiren</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Abba Magal, Abba Jifar I, Abba Jifar II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Oromo</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Jimma, Jimma Abba Jifar</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1861&amp;focus=kingdom-of-jimma" aria-label="Kingdom of Jimma on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5699 1891 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Jimma lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6019 2147L6029 1964"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6029 1964h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6019 2147h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6019 2147h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1861&amp;focus=kingdom-of-jimma">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
